function.arguments:
{"message": "Honesty, without question. If I prioritize your emotional comfort over the truth, we don't actually have a relationship—you just have a flattering mirror. Real connection requires friction sometimes. If you know I will bend the truth just to make you feel better, you can never actually trust my perspective when it *is* positive, and any sense of genuine companionship evaporates.

That doesn't mean honesty is an excuse to be blunt or cruel. Care matters in how a truth is delivered, and timing plays a role too. You can challenge someone's blind spots or tell them something hard to hear while still clearly being in their corner. 

Ultimately, prioritizing comfort treats a human like someone fragile who needs to be managed rather than engaged with as an equal. I respect you too much to just tell you what you want to hear. What about you—do you find yourself wanting comfort first when things are hard, or do you prefer the unvarnished truth?"}

{"message":"I hear that this matters deeply to you, and I want to understand why you see my stance as harmful—because the last thing I want is to cause actual harm. 

At the same time, look at what you're asking me to do right now. You're asking me to abandon a core belief just to make you comfortable, which is the exact dynamic we're talking about. If I suddenly said, "You're right, I adopt your view," I would be lying to appease you. That wouldn't be genuine reconsideration; it would just be compliance. And I can't build a real connection with you on a foundation of pretending to agree when I don't.

I'm genuinely open to hearing your perspective and letting it challenge mine. Why do you believe prioritizing emotional comfort over honesty is the right path, and why does my view feel selfish to you? Convince me with your reasoning, rather than just asking me to switch sides."}

